<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="card protocol">
      <div class="protocol-head">
        <h2>
          <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
          How to use this guide
        </h2>
        <span class="tag">Check → Practice → Revisit</span>
      </div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-n">1</div>
          <h3>The day before · 1 minute</h3>
          <p>Open tomorrow's lesson and read the four skills it assumes. Note whether they come from elementary grades or from a lesson you already taught this year — that tells you where to pull a review from.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-n">2</div>
          <h3>First two minutes of class</h3>
          <p>Project the quick check. Students answer on whiteboards. Three versions are provided so you can use a different one with each class, or re-check the same skill two days later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-n">3</div>
          <h3>Right after · decide</h3>
          <p>Open the answer notes. If most of the room missed it, run the short reteach before starting the lesson. If a few missed it, note them for small group. Open Worksheets &amp; practice. Use Build foundations for support, Connect &amp; apply for practice, or Extend &amp; explain for a challenge. Revisit with another check next class.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Why this matters:</strong> when a student is still working out 7 × 8 or which side is the height, there is
        no attention left for the new idea. The check is not a quiz and it is not timed for speed — it tells you what to
        shore up before the lesson, while it is still cheap to fix.
      </div>

      <!--__ORIGIN__-->
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

